<decompilertest>
<binaryimage arch="ARM:LE:32:Cortex">
<!--
    GH-1344. Stage P3, heritage guards: the exitless-loop guard
    (p3_dataflow/kuna_haltloopguard.rs), always on.

    Heritage reads every global at each RETURN, and that read is what keeps a
    store to a global alive through dead-code removal. A function that ends in
    a loop it never leaves has no RETURN, so the store had no reader and was
    deleted.

    sysreset (0x8020500) is the issue's 34-byte raw Thumb NVIC_SystemReset:

        movw r0,#0xed0c; movt r0,#0xe000; dsb sy
        ldr r1,[r0]; movs r2,#4; and r1,r1,#0x700; movt r2,#0x5fa
        orrs r1,r2; str r1,[r0]; dsb sy
        b .

    park (0x8000054, clang 14 -O2 thumbv7em) stores 9 to a RAM global and then
    sleeps in a wfi loop forever:

        movw r0,#0; movt r0,#0x2000; movs r1,#9; str r1,[r0]
        wfi; b <wfi>

    toggle (0x8000080) flips a GPIO output bit forever. The loop reads the
    register, but only to write it back, so the read keeps nothing alive:

        movw r1,#0x100c; movt r1,#0x4001
        ldr r2,[r1]; eor r2,r2,#0x2000; str r2,[r1]; b <ldr>

    irred (0x80000a0) is an irreducible loop entered at either store. Once dead
    code is gone, each of its two blocks holds nothing but a join, so a mark on
    a join was dropped when its block was removed:

        movw r3,#0; movt r3,#0x2000; cbnz r0,L2
        L1: movs r2,#1; str r2,[r3,#4]
        L2: movs r2,#2; str r2,[r3,#4]; b L1

    The guard keeps the AIRCR write between the two barriers, the RAM store
    before the wfi loop, the toggle, and the store the irreducible loop is
    entered with.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x8020500" readonly="true">
4ef60c50cef20000bff34f8f0168042201f4e061c0f2fa5211430160bff34f8f
fee7
</bytechunk>
